{
  "teamId": 139,
  "year": 2023,
  "narrative": "The 2023 Tampa Bay Rays announced themselves as a historic force almost immediately, blazing through April at a stunning 22-6 clip. Brandon Lowe's towering two-run blast on April 21st was emblematic of a lineup that could hurt you from anywhere, and the Rays took that energy into one of the best starts any team had enjoyed in recent memory. They were not just winning — they were winning with authority, combining a suffocating pitching staff anchored by a 3.86 ERA with a lineup that slugged 230 home runs on the season.\n\nMay and June brought a different kind of baseball — scrappier, more contested, and occasionally painful. Jason Adam and Pete Fairbanks suffered some high-leverage meltdowns, including Pete Alonso's devastating three-run walk-off blast off Fairbanks on May 17th that swung the win probability by nearly 80 points. Yet the Rays kept finding answers: Josh Lowe's three-run homer, Taylor Walls' grand slam, and Randy Arozarena's clutch shot in June kept Tampa Bay above water. Josh Lowe's two-run double on June 28th, worth 0.701 WPA, was perhaps the signature clutch swing of the first half. The team finished May at 17-12 and June at 17-10 — winning, even if not always pretty.\n\nJuly arrived like a gut punch. The Rays cratered to 8-16, their worst month by far, and the season's momentum seemed to evaporate under the summer heat. But August proved the 2023 Rays were not finished. Wander Franco, Brandon Lowe, Randy Arozarena, Josh Lowe, and Yandy Diaz all delivered in clutch moments as Tampa Bay roared back at 17-8. September was a grind at 16-11, but the team navigated the stretch run through a mix of big home runs and late-inning drama, holding off challengers to finish at an impressive 99-63.\n\nThe postseason, however, was a cold and swift ending. Facing the Texas Rangers in the Wild Card round, the Rays ran into a buzz saw. Game 1 saw Tyler Glasnow victimized by a deflected single off his own glove and Chris Devenski surrender a two-run hit from Corey Seager. Game 2 brought more heartbreak as Zach Eflin — otherwise outstanding in the regular season — surrendered home runs to Adolis Garcia and rookie Evan Carter, with Josh Jung adding a triple for good measure. A 99-win season that had dazzled from February onward ended on the Rangers' terms, a sobering reminder that in October, even the best teams can run out of magic.",
  "chapters": [
